k within the requested size while remaining literature-guided (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of serine to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reduced subseafloor fluids supply reducing equivalents; moderate residence time favored to limit thermal degradation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrothermal deoxygenation of the serine side-chain hydroxymethyl group gives alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aubrey et al. (2008) describe hydrothermal amino acid interconversions and specifically note serine reduction to alanine. Including this step links the surface serine pool to the hydrothermal alanine hub that feeds threonine, although moderate temperatures are preferred because hydroxy amino acids can also decompose under harsher vent conditions (Aubrey et al., 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted reductive amination of pyruvate to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental iron\", \"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"elemental iron and Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Iron-rich anoxic hydrothermal porewater; hydroxylamine serves as nitrogen source and reactive intermediate donor\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate condenses with hydroxylamine to an oxime or imino-like intermediate that is reduced by Fe0 and Fe2+ to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The review cites Muchowska et al. (2019) for iron-prom