t reductive amination that is chemically reasonable but partially speculative as written (Muchowska et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Geothermal reduction of pyruvaldehyde to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"metal sulfide surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS or NiS surfaces\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-bearing subaerial geothermal field; selective ketone reduction is uncertain\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Selective reduction of the ketone carbonyl of pyruvaldehyde gives the alpha-hydroxy aldehyde lactaldehyde while retaining the aldehyde terminus.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No direct paper in the provided set demonstrates this exact reduction, but the review explicitly identifies pyruvaldehyde reduction as one plausible route to the lactaldehyde needed for the threonine branch. It is included as a speculative supply step because lactaldehyde availability is a major open question, and selectivity against competing carbonyl reductions is uncertain (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from lactaldehyde and HCN\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanosulfidic pool chemistry; CN- addition favored during concentrated wet phases\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehydic carbonyl of lactaldehyde, and protonation of the resulting alkoxide affords the cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN addition to lactaldehyde is the entry point to threonine-type cyanosulfidic chemistry. For atom balance, the cyanohydrin is represented here as the chemically consistent C4 adduct expected from HCN addition to a C3 aldehyde; the review's printed C5 formula is treated as a summary error rather than a different substance (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonolysis of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich wet phases; avoid prolonged UV exposure after aminonitrile formation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-hydroxynitrile is converted to the corresponding aminonitrile through ammonia-promoted substitution or imine-like capture at the cyanohydrin center.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ammonia conversion of the lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile follows the reported Patel sequence. The step is plausible in ammonia-rich pools, although aminonitrile persistence under UV exposure and fluctuating pH remains uncertain (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker formation of threonine aminonitrile from lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"One-pot Strecker-like condensation in concentrated surface pools; expected racemic and possibly diastereomeric product mixture\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactaldehyde forms an imine or iminium with ammonia, followed by cyanide addition to give the alpha-aminonitrile directly.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. isolated the stepwise cyanohydrin route rather than this one-pot condensation, so this reaction is explicitly speculative. It is nevertheless chemically reasonable as a direct Strecker condensation of lactaldehyde under concentrated NH3/HCN conditions and should converge on the same aminonitrile framework (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Net hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile to threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm aqueous hydrolysis; net reaction consumes two water equivalents via an implicit amide\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrated to an amide and then to the carboxylic acid, giving threonine with net release of ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile to threonine is the expected terminal nitrile-to-acid conversion in the cyanosulfidic framework. The network writes the overall hydrolysis as a net step with the amide implicit; two water equivalents are consumed overall, and competing aminonitrile loss remains a caveat (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal homologation of alanine with formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueo